Read this as: v0->v1 is pure engineering (same bytes, fixed access
pattern). v1->v2->v3->v4 is bytes-moved reduction, each step trading
some accuracy (rightmost column) for bandwidth. v5-v7 move the SAME
bytes as v2/v3/v4 respectively -- any speedup there is purely from
better latency hiding (v5's extra threads/row, v6's DP4A instruction,
v7's independent accumulators), not from reading less data. If v5-v7
don't beat their v2/v3/v4 counterparts on your GPU, that's a real
result too: it means those kernels were already latency-hidden well
enough that the extra complexity wasn't the bottleneck.

