Read the sweep as: at B=1-4 GB/s stays near the B=1 fp16 level (weights read
once per launch) and throughput(req/s) climbs ~linearly in B -- the cheap win
of continuous batching. From B=8 on, every output row has to touch the whole
batched activation vector (in_dim x B floats), and that x traffic -- plus
SIMT instruction issue, not weight bandwidth or FLOPs -- becomes the wall:
watch throughput per request (req/s / B) fall off and %peak sag. The designs
in this file all stop at the same wall from different angles (v11: x re-read
per row from L2; v12: in_dim address computations per lane; v13: smem loads
per (column, entry) pair). What breaks through it is tensor-core GEMM -- the
same tiling as v13 with m16n8k16 MMAs reusing each x row across 16 output
rows per instruction -- which is what cuBLAS / vLLM actually run at large B.
